<!-- Example -->
<group name="local,syslog,sshd,">

  <!--
  Dec 10 01:02:02 host sshd[1234]: Failed none for root from 1.1.1.1 port 1066 ssh2
  -->
  <rule id="100001" level="5">
    <if_sid>5716</if_sid>
    <srcip>1.1.1.1</srcip>
    <description>sshd: authentication failed from IP 1.1.1.1.</description>
    <group>authentication_failed,pci_dss_10.2.4,pci_dss_10.2.5,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<!--
  Zeek/ICSNPP Modbus events - see docs/zeek-network-mirroring-design.md.
  Collection (the <localfile> entries in zeek/Dockerfile) only gets these
  events to the manager; without a rule, they're received and then
  silently dropped (logall_json is off by default) - a rule is what
  actually turns them into visible alerts.

  100100/100101 are level 1, not 0 - this matters more than it looks.
  Level 0 ("ignore") rules are excluded from Wazuh's frequency/
  if_matched_sid correlation entirely, not just from alert output -
  confirmed directly with wazuh-logtest: a rule depending on
  if_matched_sid against a level-0 rule never fires, no matter how many
  times the base rule matches. <options>no_log</options> looks like the
  fix (the name suggests "match, but don't log") but doesn't suppress
  output either - also confirmed directly, still produced full alerts.
  The actual mechanism: the manager's global <log_alert_level> (ossec.conf,
  default 3) is the real output floor, independent of correlation. Level 1
  is low enough to stay under that floor (no output, no indexing) while
  still being non-zero, which is all correlation needs.

  100101 requires matched=true - this closes a real false-positive path
  tied to the modbus_pending expiration gap recorded (and, at the time,
  believed not load-bearing) in the design doc's §5.10: unmatched pending
  requests in ICSNPP's table apparently do eventually get flushed out
  individually, but keep their *original* request timestamp in "ts" while
  being written to the log at whatever later wall-clock moment they
  actually flush - confirmed live, a single long-running connection
  (15+ hours uptime) periodically re-emitted the exact same stale,
  matched=false event over and over, minutes to hours apart, and a
  cluster of these flushing close together was enough to cross the
  frequency threshold in §100103 as a false positive. Requiring
  matched=true excludes these - a genuine attacker burst is always a
  completed, matched request/response exchange in real time, so this
  costs nothing on the detection side.
-->
<group name="modbus,ics,zeek,">

  <rule id="100100" level="1">
    <location>/usr/local/zeek/logs/modbus_detailed.log</location>
    <description>Zeek: Modbus protocol event (categorization only, not alerted)</description>
    <group>modbus,ics,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100101" level="1">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="func">WRITE</field>
    <field name="matched">true</field>
    <description>Zeek: Modbus write command (categorization only, not alerted)</description>
    <group>modbus,ics,ics_write,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Writes targeting the PLC itself (192.168.95.2) - its manual-mode
    registers (coil 0, holding registers 10-13; see
    docs/cyber-vs-physical-fault-injection-design.md §4.4's stuck-valve
    pairing). Measured live against 133s of real normal operation: 301
    legitimate read-only events reached the PLC, zero writes. Automatic
    control never writes to the PLC over Modbus - only a real maintenance
    action or an attack would - so this needs no frequency/threshold
    logic at all. "It happened" already is the signal. Validated live
    against the real attack: attacker/cyber_injects/setpoint_inject.py
    run from Kali against the real PLC fired this rule immediately and
    correctly for both the register write and the manual-mode coil write,
    with the true source (192.168.90.6) correctly attributed.
  -->
  <rule id="100102" level="12">
    <if_sid>100101</if_sid>
    <field name="id.resp_h">^192\.168\.95\.2$</field>
    <description>Zeek: Unauthorized Modbus write to PLC $(id.resp_h), address $(address) - PLC manual-mode registers are never written during normal automatic operation</description>
    <group>modbus,ics,ics_write,ics_unauthorized_write,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Write-rate anomaly on the PLC<->remote-IO polling channel (separate
    from 100102 - this is the PLC's own normal control-loop traffic, not
    writes targeting the PLC). No existing cyber inject attacks this
    specific channel, so there's no real attack to validate this against
    yet - included as a template for future scenarios there.

    Threshold calibrated from two live measurements, not a guess - the
    first one was wrong and worth recording why. An initial 133s sample
    showed a steady ~8.5 writes/sec per remote-IO target, peaking at ~21
    writes in any 2-second window, so the threshold was first set to 40
    (~2x that peak) - same_field grouping confirmed working correctly
    (matches distributed evenly across all 6 targets), but it still fired
    constantly against real, unmodified baseline traffic. A second,
    longer, re-verified-clean measurement (log truncated immediately
    before, zero test traffic during) showed why: 5 of 6 targets are
    tight (max ~19-20/2s), but one (192.168.95.13) genuinely runs ~3x
    higher (max ~60/2s) - a real, reproducible structural difference
    between that target and the other five, not measurement noise (cause
    not fully diagnosed - a single persistent connection, same as the
    others, just a different read/write cadence).

    Since same_field applies one threshold across every grouped value,
    it has to clear the *worst* target's baseline, not the typical one:
    set to 120 (2x 192.168.95.13's observed peak), confirmed via a clean
    2-minute window with zero new matches at this threshold. Known
    tradeoff: this sacrifices sensitivity for the 5 quieter targets, whose
    own real baseline would tolerate a much lower threshold - a genuine
    burst to one of those could reach 2-3x its own normal rate without
    reaching 120. Validated against a real burst regardless: a synthetic
    ~2,300 writes/sec burst (from a separate, genuinely mirrored container,
    not an intra-container loopback - see the design doc for why that
    distinction mattered) fired this rule correctly and immediately, with
    the actual source IP attributed. Per-target thresholds (a separate
    rule per target, more precise but more complex) are a possible
    follow-up, not built this round.
  -->
  <rule id="100103" level="10" frequency="120" timeframe="2">
    <if_matched_sid>100101</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>id.resp_h</same_field>
    <description>Zeek: Modbus write-rate anomaly - more than 120 writes to $(id.resp_h) within 2 seconds</description>
    <group>modbus,ics,ics_write,ics_write_burst,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<!--
  Zeek http.log - PLC programming over the OpenPLC web UI (192.168.95.2:8080).
  A program change from the EWS never crosses the router, so Suricata can't
  see it; only the Zeek mirror can. OpenPLC's flow is POST /upload-program
  (the .st file) -> /upload-program-action (register it) -> GET
  /compile-program?file=... (stops the runtime, compiles, loads it). Pairs
  with the EWS file-integrity rules (100230-100232): the file changed there,
  and here is where it went.
-->
<group name="zeek,http,ics,plc_programming,">

  <rule id="100110" level="0">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <location>/usr/local/zeek/logs/http.log</location>
    <description>Zeek: HTTP request (categorization only)</description>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100111" level="12">
    <if_sid>100110</if_sid>
    <field name="id.resp_h">^192\.168\.95\.2$</field>
    <field name="method">^POST$</field>
    <field name="uri">^/upload-program$</field>
    <description>Zeek: PLC program uploaded to $(id.resp_h) from $(id.orig_h)</description>
    <group>plc_program_upload,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100112" level="12">
    <if_sid>100110</if_sid>
    <field name="id.resp_h">^192\.168\.95\.2$</field>
    <field name="uri">^/compile-program</field>
    <description>Zeek: PLC program compiled and loaded on $(id.resp_h) from $(id.orig_h) - $(uri)</description>
    <group>plc_program_load,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100113" level="10">
    <if_sid>100110</if_sid>
    <field name="id.resp_h">^192\.168\.95\.2$</field>
    <field name="uri">^/start_plc|^/stop_plc</field>
    <description>Zeek: PLC runtime $(uri) on $(id.resp_h) from $(id.orig_h)</description>
    <group>plc_runtime_change,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<!--
  Router DNS log (dnsmasq query lines, decoded by wazuh/local_decoder.xml).

  Every query is alerted at level 3 - the manager's log_alert_level floor -
  so all lookups are searchable in Wazuh (the router UI only shows the last
  50). DNS on an OT network is small and predictable, so anything outside
  the expected list below stands out at level 8:
    cti.wazuh.com    - the Wazuh manager's own threat-intel feed
    javamelody.org   - ScadaLTS's bundled monitoring library on the HMI
    wazuh[.*]        - agents resolving "wazuh"; the laptop's DNS search
                       domain gets appended, so the suffix varies per student
    *.in-addr.arpa   - reverse lookups, handled by the burst rule instead
  A burst of reverse lookups from one host is what nmap does after a sweep
  (it resolves every live host it finds) - the Chapter 3 scan.
-->
<group name="dns,dnsmasq,grfics,">

  <rule id="100210" level="3">
    <decoded_as>dnsmasq-query</decoded_as>
    <description>DNS query: $(query) ($(qtype)) from $(srcip)</description>
    <group>dns_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100211" level="8">
    <if_sid>100210</if_sid>
    <field name="query" type="pcre2" negate="yes">^(cti\.wazuh\.com|javamelody\.org|wazuh(\..+)?|.+\.in-addr\.arpa)$</field>
    <description>DNS query for an unexpected name: $(query) from $(srcip)</description>
    <group>dns_query,dns_unexpected,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100212" level="3">
    <if_sid>100210</if_sid>
    <field name="qtype">^PTR$</field>
    <description>DNS reverse lookup: $(query) from $(srcip)</description>
    <group>dns_query,dns_ptr,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ignore: one sweep is hundreds of lookups; without it this re-fires
       every 6 (42 alerts for one /24 sweep in testing). -->
  <rule id="100213" level="10" frequency="6" timeframe="10" ignore="120">
    <if_matched_sid>100212</if_matched_sid>
    <same_srcip />
    <description>DNS reverse-lookup burst from $(srcip) - likely a network scan resolving the hosts it found</description>
    <group>dns_query,dns_ptr_burst,recon,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<!--
  Router ARP monitor (router/arpmon.py, JSON events). arpmon only sees the
  router's own ARP table, so it catches spoofing that targets the router -
  e.g. the HMI <-> gateway MitM, which has to poison the router to see both
  directions.

  changed_mac alone also fires when a container is recreated (new MAC), so
  it stays at 10. When the new MAC already answers for a different IP
  (mac_also_claimed_by, set by arpmon) it's the classic spoofing signature -
  the attacker's own MAC claiming the victim's IP - so that's 12.
-->
<group name="arp,arpmon,grfics,">

  <rule id="100220" level="0">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <location>/var/log/arpmon/events.json</location>
    <description>arpmon event (categorization only)</description>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100221" level="5">
    <if_sid>100220</if_sid>
    <field name="event">^new_station$</field>
    <description>ARP: new device $(ip) ($(mac)) seen on $(iface)</description>
    <group>arp_new_station,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100222" level="10">
    <if_sid>100220</if_sid>
    <field name="event">^changed_mac$</field>
    <description>ARP: $(ip) moved from MAC $(old_mac) to $(mac) on $(iface) - possible ARP spoofing, or a replaced/recreated device</description>
    <group>arp_changed_mac,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100223" level="12">
    <if_sid>100222</if_sid>
    <field name="mac_also_claimed_by">\.+</field>
    <description>ARP spoofing: $(ip) now maps to MAC $(mac), which also answers for $(mac_also_claimed_by)</description>
    <group>arp_changed_mac,arp_spoofing,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100224" level="12">
    <if_sid>100221</if_sid>
    <field name="mac_also_claimed_by">\.+</field>
    <description>ARP: new address $(ip) uses MAC $(mac), which already answers for $(mac_also_claimed_by) - possible spoofing</description>
    <group>arp_new_station,arp_spoofing,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<!--
  PLC project file integrity on the EWS (workstation/Dockerfile adds a
  realtime, report_changes syscheck entry for /home/engineer/Desktop,
  restricted to .st/.xml). The stock rules alert on these at level 5-7
  like any file change; a change to the PLC program is what precedes a
  malicious logic download (course Chapter 7), so raise it. Legitimate
  engineering changes fire too - that's the point: every logic change
  should line up with an approved change ticket.
-->
<group name="syscheck,plc_project,grfics,">

  <rule id="100230" level="12">
    <if_sid>550</if_sid>
    <field name="file">^/home/engineer/Desktop/</field>
    <description>PLC project file modified on the EWS: $(file)</description>
    <group>plc_logic_change,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100231" level="10">
    <if_sid>554</if_sid>
    <field name="file">^/home/engineer/Desktop/</field>
    <description>PLC project file added on the EWS: $(file)</description>
    <group>plc_logic_change,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100232" level="10">
    <if_sid>553</if_sid>
    <field name="file">^/home/engineer/Desktop/</field>
    <description>PLC project file deleted on the EWS: $(file)</description>
    <group>plc_logic_change,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
